course: dictionary
lesson: 2294
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their job depending on the sentence. The word criminal can be a noun, an adjective, or an informal word, and its meaning changes with the job it does. Look at the words around it to find which meaning fits.
explain: A criminal (noun) is a person who has broken the law. «Police catch criminals» means the police arrest people who have done crimes. Criminal (adjective) means to do with crime, as in «criminal law» or «criminal damage».
explain: Informal speech adds a third meaning: very wrong, wasteful or unfair. «It's criminal to waste all that food» does not mean the food is a crime. It means the waste is shocking. Ask yourself: is this sentence about a person, about crime, or about how bad something is?
fact: A criminal (noun) is a person who has broken the law and done a crime.
fact: Criminal (adjective) means to do with crime or with the law about crime, as in «a criminal record».
fact: Informal: «criminal» can mean very wrong, wasteful or unfair, as in «It's criminal to waste all that food.»
fact: Phrases to know: «a dangerous criminal», «criminal offence», «criminal damage».
fact: «Ransomware» is called criminal software: it locks files and demands payment.
exercise: «The police chased the criminal through the park.» What is a criminal here? | *A person who has broken the law | A kind of park bench | A fast-moving cloud | A police officer on a break
exercise: «It's criminal to waste all that food.» What does criminal mean here? | A person who steals food | Hungry and tired | Open and friendly | *Very wrong and wasteful
exercise: «A jury is twelve ordinary adults who listen to a criminal trial.» Here criminal is an adjective. What does it describe? | A trial for a sports team | A trial with lots of noise | *A trial about a crime | A trial held in a school
test: In «criminal damage», criminal is an adjective. What does it mean? | Very wrong and unfair | *To do with crime | Very old | A person who has broken the law
test: «Hacker» once meant a clever, playful programmer; today the news often uses it for a computer criminal. What is a computer criminal? | A teacher of computers | A programmer who wins prizes | A clever, playful child | *A person who breaks into computers to commit crime
exam: In «a criminal record», what does criminal mean? | A list of games someone has played | A music album of old songs | *A list of crimes someone was found guilty of | A record-breaking race result
